<section style="background:var(--card);border-block:1px solid var(--line)"><div class="wrap">
<div class="about">
<div><span class="eyebrow">BCIN</span>
<h2>What is a BCIN Qualified Designer?</h2></div>
<div>
<p>A BCIN is a Building Code Identification Number issued in Ontario. Designers who hold one have passed the provincial exams for the categories they practise in, and can prepare and submit drawings for a building permit in those categories.</p>
<p>For houses and many small buildings, the Ontario Building Code allows the drawings to be prepared by a BCIN Qualified Designer. Larger or more complex buildings require an architect.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div></section>

<section><div class="wrap">
<div class="sec-head"><div><span class="eyebrow">What is included</span>
<h2>From measurements to permit</h2></div>
<p class="lead">The drawing set depends on the project. These are the steps most permit projects go through with us.</p></div>
<ol class="svc">
<li><span class="n">01</span><h3>Site visit and measurements</h3><p>We measure the existing house or space and note what can and cannot change.</p></li>
<li><span class="n">02</span><h3>Design</h3><p>Layouts and options until the plan works for you, before the permit set is drawn.</p></li>
<li><span class="n">03</span><h3>Permit drawings</h3><p>Plans, elevations, sections and details as the project requires, prepared to Ontario Building Code requirements.</p></li>
<li><span class="n">04</span><h3>Submission and review</h3><p>We help you submit the application and support you through the municipality's review.</p></li>
<li><span class="n">05</span><h3>Construction drawings</h3><p>Clear drawings for contractors to price and build from, so quotes compare the same job.</p></li>
</ol>
</div></section>

<section style="background:var(--card);border-block:1px solid var(--line)"><div class="wrap">
<div class="sec-head"><div><span class="eyebrow">Projects</span>
<h2>Typical permit projects</h2></div></div>
<div class="about" style="margin-top:28px">
<p>Renovations that move or remove walls, add or move plumbing, or change how a space is used; additions; new custom homes; and fit-outs of retail spaces in small buildings.</p>
<p>Not sure if your project needs a permit? Tell us what you want to change and we will let you know at the free consultation. See also our <a href="/services/home-renovation-toronto/">full-home renovation</a> service.</p>
</div>
</div></section>

<section id="faq"><div class="wrap">
<span class="eyebrow">FAQ</span>
<h2>Permit questions</h2>
<div style="margin-top:28px">
<details><summary>Do I need a permit for my renovation?</summary><p>Many projects do, especially those that change the structure, add space, move or add plumbing, or change how a space is used. Cosmetic work usually does not. We tell you early which approvals your project needs.</p></details>
<details><summary>Do I need an architect or can a BCIN designer do my drawings?</summary><p>For houses and many small buildings, the Ontario Building Code allows the drawings to be prepared by a BCIN Qualified Designer. Larger or more complex buildings require an architect.</p></details>
<details><summary>How long does a building permit take in Toronto?</summary><p>Review times depend on the municipality, the type of project and how complete the application is. A complete, clear drawing set helps avoid delays during the review.</p></details>
</div></div></section>

